.TH MONSWCTL 1M "31 October 2026" "monsw"
.SH NAME
monswctl \- read and set the monitor switch, and switch the screen
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B monswctl
[
.B \-d
.I dev
]
.B get
|
.B status
|
.BI set " value"
|
.B rtg
[
.I command
[
.I argument ...
] ]
|
.B native
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B monswctl
talks to the monitor switch,
.BR monsw (7),
through its device,
.B /dev/monsw
(or
.IR dev ).
Opening the device loads the
.B monsw
module when it is not loaded yet.
.TP
.B get
prints the five fields the driver reports, one per line:
.BR config ,
the value in effect and its meaning;
.BR source ,
where it came from
.RB ( none ,
.BR seed ,
.BR set ,
.BR patched );
.BR state ,
.BR native ,
.B rtg
or
.B none
(not switched yet);
.BR holder ,
1 when a program holds the switch on the card's picture;
.BR ncards ,
the card drivers registered.
.TP
.B status
prints the same in one line.
.TP
.BI set " value"
makes
.I value
the value in effect, unless one is patched into the kernel; root only.
.I value
is a sum of the bits of
.BR monsw (7),
in decimal or as
.BR 0x \&
hex;
.B \-1
unsets it, so that the card's own value applies again.
A value with any other bit is refused here, before the kernel sees it.
The boot step
.B /etc/rc2.d/S20monsw
uses this to put the value from
.BR /etc/display
into the kernel at every boot.
.TP
.B rtg
switches the monitor to the card's picture, runs
.I command
and switches back when it ends, however it ends.
Without a command it holds the card's picture until the end of its input
or until it is killed.
Its exit status is the command's.
.TP
.B native
switches the monitor back to the chipset's picture.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 done; 1 the device or the kernel refused, with one line that says why;
2 a usage mistake, before the device is opened.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /dev/monsw
the device, character major 52, minor 0.
.TP
.B /etc/display
the
.B monsw
line, the value set at boot; see
.BR display (4).
.TP
.B /etc/monsw
the older file: one line, the value.
The boot step reads it only when
.B /etc/display
has no
.B monsw
line, and the boot log says so.
.TP
.B /var/adm/rc.log
the boot log: one
.B monsw:
line for each boot.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
.ft CW
monswctl status
monsw: value 34 (SEL low=RTG) from set; state native; holder no; 1 card
monswctl rtg sleep 10
.ft R
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR monsw (7),
.BR display (4),
.BR display.d (4),
.BR xstart (1),
.BR rc.subr (1M)
